 This problem can be solved using the binomial probability formula. The binomial probability formula is given by: \[
P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}
\] where \(\binom{n}{k} = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\) is the binomial coefficient, \(n\) is the number of trials (plate appearances), \(k\) is the number of successes (walks), and \(p\) is the probability of success on a single trial (earning a walk). In this case, \(n = 2\), \(k = 1\), and \(p = \frac{2}{5}\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get: \[
P(X = 1) = \binom{2}{1} \left(\frac{2}{5}\right)^1 \left(1 - \frac{2}{5}\right)^{2-1} = 2 \cdot \frac{2}{5} \cdot \frac{3}{5} = \frac{12}{25}
\] </think> <answer> \(\frac{12}{25}\) </answer>

