\text{proj}_{\mathbf{v_1}} \mathbf{v_2} = \frac{\mathbf{v_1} \cdot \mathbf{v_2}}{\mathbf{v_1} \cdot \mathbf{v_1}} \mathbf{v_1}
\] where \(\mathbf{v_1} \cdot \mathbf{v_2} = 4 \cdot (-2) + 4 \cdot 2 = 0\) and \(\mathbf{v_1} \cdot \mathbf{v_1} = 4 \cdot 4 + 4 \cdot 4 = 32\). So, \(\text{proj}_{\mathbf{v_1}} \mathbf{v_2} = \frac{0}{32} \mathbf{v_1} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\). However, the projection takes \(\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}\) to \(\begin{pmatrix} \frac{60}{13} \\ \frac{12}{13} \end{pmatrix}\), so we need to find a projection matrix \(P\) such that \(P \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{60}{13} \\ \frac{12}{13} \end{pmatrix}\). The projection matrix \(P\) is given by \[
P = \frac{1}{32} \begin{pmatrix} 32 & 0 \\ 0 & 32 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & 0 \\ 0 & \frac{1}{2} \end{pmatrix}
